WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:02.202
Hey, great to be back on CNN.

00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:06.506
We have Texas senator author
of<i> Unwoke,</i> Ted Cruz.

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:07.507
Okay.

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:10.643
And former psychology professor
Jordan Peterson,

00:00:10.776 --> 00:00:13.646
and opinion columnist
for<i> New York Times,</i> Pamela Paul.

00:00:13.779 --> 00:00:14.647
(AUDIENCE APPLAUDING)

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:18.251
Okay, so... here we go.

00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:19.652
This first one is for you.

00:00:19.786 --> 00:00:22.655
Do Republicans
have a good answer
to address the border crisis

00:00:22.756 --> 00:00:25.124
other than bussing migrants
to liberal cities?

00:00:25.224 --> 00:00:26.625
Oh, a little dig there, Ted.

00:00:26.725 --> 00:00:28.428
-A little...
-(CHUCKLING)

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:31.965
It's actually very simple,
which is secure the border.

00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:34.400
And when you apprehend someone,
send them home.

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:39.305
When Joe Biden started,
we had the lowest rate of
illegal immigration in 45 years.

00:00:39.638 --> 00:00:42.741 line:0%
And he came in, and he
immediately reimposed
catch-and-release,

00:00:42.841 --> 00:00:46.613 line:0%
and it produced the worst rate
of illegal immigration
in our nation's history.

00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:47.947 line:0%
I spend a lot of time
at the border.

00:00:48.047 --> 00:00:50.850 line:0%
I go out on midnight patrol
with border patrol agents.

00:00:50.984 --> 00:00:51.851
-The problem--
-Really?

00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:53.152
Many times.
You should come.

00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:54.487
I'll actually bring you
if you're interested in--

00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:56.022
-No, I'm good.
-(LAUGHTER)

00:00:56.088 --> 00:00:58.158
-If you want to see--
-I'm good. No, no.

00:00:58.258 --> 00:00:59.792
I will tell you this,
actually, Bill,

00:00:59.926 --> 00:01:02.595
if you saw what was happening,
you would be horrified,

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:05.431
because the people being
abused by the traffickers--

00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:08.434
You don't understand it 'til you
see it firsthand, and it is--

00:01:08.501 --> 00:01:10.770
it is immoral what's happening
at our southern border.

00:01:10.903 --> 00:01:12.605
-I take your word.
-Okay.

00:01:12.705 --> 00:01:13.739
I don't have to go.

00:01:13.839 --> 00:01:15.809
(LAUGHTER)

00:01:15.942 --> 00:01:17.644
-But it is easy to fix...
-Okay, but--

00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:20.580
...because what actually worked

00:01:20.913 --> 00:01:24.484
is we had an agreement
with Mexico that was called
the Remain in Mexico agreement,

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:27.286
which says that if someone
crossed into Mexico illegally,

00:01:27.419 --> 00:01:29.489
from typically Central
or South America,

00:01:29.856 --> 00:01:34.360
they would remain in Mexico
while their asylum case
was proceeding in the US.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:35.928
And the numbers plummeted.

00:01:36.028 --> 00:01:38.331
We had the lowest rate,
as I said, in 45 years.

00:01:38.464 --> 00:01:42.568
And then Biden came in
and ripped that international
agreement to shreds.

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:46.673
-And that's what produced now
8.4 million illegal aliens...
-Okay. Okay.

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:47.673
...since Biden's been president.

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:49.842
Boy, I walked right into
that one, didn't I?

00:01:50.376 --> 00:01:51.411
-TED CRUZ: You asked.
-BILL MAHER: I know--

00:01:51.544 --> 00:01:53.413
I know you know this,
this is part of your...

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:56.616
But, all right, let me ask it
a different way.

00:01:56.749 --> 00:02:00.319
Do you think if we attacked
it from the reverse angle,

00:02:00.419 --> 00:02:03.923
-in other words, if we punish
the employers, it would--
-Absolutely.

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:04.924
And you think we should?

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:06.525
Oh, look, I am all for E-Verify.

00:02:06.625 --> 00:02:08.294
I would absolutely
punish the employers.

00:02:08.394 --> 00:02:10.696
And I've introduced legislation
to do that multiple times.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:11.698
And what happened?

00:02:12.232 --> 00:02:14.366
Your party must not like that,
because we know
cheap labor is good.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.336
So look, there are a lot of
Republicans who don't like that.

00:02:17.436 --> 00:02:20.973
There are fewer,
but I do think that the
Republican Party is changing.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.475
I think we're becoming much more
of a blue-collar party,

00:02:23.609 --> 00:02:25.011
which I think
is a very good thing.

00:02:25.178 --> 00:02:29.114
-Okay. This is for... (LAUGHS)
-(LAUGHTER)

00:02:29.348 --> 00:02:30.649
They are switching,
aren't they?

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:35.988
Yes, it's interesting that the
parties are kind of switching,
like, who the elitists are.

00:02:36.088 --> 00:02:38.524
It used to be
the country club Republicans,

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:41.160
and now it's the
Chardonnay-sipping Democrat.

00:02:41.294 --> 00:02:42.462
-Yeah.
-PAMELA PAUL: Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:42.562 --> 00:02:43.563
I know you've written about it.

00:02:43.663 --> 00:02:45.999
It's an interesting do-si-do
the parties do.

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:48.201
It's almost happened completely
in Canada now.

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:50.603
The Conservatives are basically
the working class party
in Canada.

00:02:50.737 --> 00:02:51.737
-MAHER: Right.
-Yeah.

00:02:51.837 --> 00:02:53.105
But the heart of
the Republican Party

00:02:53.205 --> 00:02:56.275
are truck drivers
and steel workers
and cops and firefighters.

00:02:56.442 --> 00:02:58.544
And I think that
is a fantastic shift.

00:02:58.644 --> 00:03:00.579
I think we should be
the party of jobs,

00:03:00.746 --> 00:03:02.081
-we should be the party of--
-Okay.

00:03:02.181 --> 00:03:03.482
...of people
who want to work hard.

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:04.683
Okay, okay.

00:03:04.783 --> 00:03:09.088
(LAUGHTER)

00:03:10.956 --> 00:03:14.994
For Jordan,
are men more susceptible
to loneliness than women?

00:03:15.428 --> 00:03:16.396
No.

00:03:17.597 --> 00:03:19.465
-No, well-- well--
-MAHER: Okay.

00:03:19.765 --> 00:03:20.733
We know this.

00:03:21.333 --> 00:03:25.504 line:0%
So, at puberty, women
become more sensitive
to negative emotion than men.

00:03:25.604 --> 00:03:28.074 line:0%
And then, that's permanent,
on average,

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:29.775 line:0%
through the entire life course.

00:03:30.109 --> 00:03:32.544 line:0%
And it looks like
it's associated
with hormonal changes.

00:03:32.644 --> 00:03:35.615 line:0%
And so, cross-culturally,
women experience higher levels

00:03:35.682 --> 00:03:37.716
of depression and anxiety
and shame and guilt,

00:03:37.817 --> 00:03:39.919
all the negative emotions
that clump together.

00:03:40.152 --> 00:03:43.289
And so, loneliness
is a pain-related emotion,

00:03:43.456 --> 00:03:45.157
and women are more
susceptible to that.

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:46.159
What do you-- Now we--

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:51.497
Everybody recognizes that women,
girls, mature faster than boys.

00:03:51.664 --> 00:03:53.766
I mean,
this is why they're going
out with the upper classmen.

00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:57.370
And at any age, they usually
don't want somebody
exactly their age,

00:03:57.537 --> 00:04:00.672
even though this country is like
crazy for age appropriate--

00:04:00.806 --> 00:04:02.508
God forbid, we go out of that.

00:04:02.808 --> 00:04:05.144
(LAUGHTER)

00:04:05.377 --> 00:04:09.748
But, what do you think,
if you had to name a number,

00:04:10.249 --> 00:04:14.353
of like how far ahead
the women are years-wise.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.356
Well, we know women prefer
men four years older
cross-culturally.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:18.991
-MAHER: Four.
-Yeah. Yeah, yeah.

00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:21.127
-But--
-And it's not necessarily only

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:22.595
a matter of maturation.

00:04:22.729 --> 00:04:25.898
It's also the case that women
are more attracted by--

00:04:26.499 --> 00:04:29.302
by socio-economic status
and productivity

00:04:29.402 --> 00:04:30.770
than men are in
relationship with women.

00:04:30.903 --> 00:04:33.973
How many years head-start
does a man need

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:37.176
to match his maturity level
to a woman's level?

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:38.511
I think 40.

00:04:38.711 --> 00:04:41.013
(LAUGHTER)

00:04:41.213 --> 00:04:42.381
JORDAN PETERSON:
Well, there's wide--

00:04:42.482 --> 00:04:45.018
wide individual differences
in that too, Bill.

00:04:45.951 --> 00:04:47.386
PAUL: I'm learning a lot
about women here.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:48.820
MAHER: Am I wrong about that?

00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:50.323
(CHUCKLING)

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.959
-Well I-- I'm-- Yeah.
-MAHER: Okay.

00:04:53.392 --> 00:04:56.595
I would say I have a different
view of women from Jordan.

00:04:56.996 --> 00:04:58.564
But, what do you mean different?

00:04:58.664 --> 00:05:03.436
Well, I mean,
the depression, anxiety--

00:05:04.336 --> 00:05:05.938 line:0%
What else do we have that's...?

00:05:06.004 --> 00:05:06.939 line:0%
-PETERSON: Negative--
-Loneliness.

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.007 line:0%
-PETERSON: Negative emotions.
-Everything negative--

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:09.842 line:0%
PETERSON: No, no, no.
No, not everything negative.

00:05:09.975 --> 00:05:12.278 line:0%
-Okay.
-Just because men are much more
likely to be aggressive,

00:05:12.378 --> 00:05:14.613
and to end up in prison,
and to be alcoholic,

00:05:14.713 --> 00:05:18.017
and they're overrepresented
in learning disabilities.

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:20.819
Like, there there are sex
differences in psychopathology,

00:05:20.953 --> 00:05:24.390
and they're not all tilted
negatively towards women.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:25.491
Definitely not.

00:05:25.992 --> 00:05:29.162
So, but negative emotion,
it's absolutely clear
that that's the case.

00:05:29.262 --> 00:05:31.964
And it's-- The biggest
differences are in the countries

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:36.601
that have the most
gender-equal economies
and socio-economic structures.

00:05:36.902 --> 00:05:38.704
So, the differences
between men and women

00:05:38.838 --> 00:05:40.473
in terms of sensitivity
to negative emotion

00:05:40.639 --> 00:05:42.809
maximize in Scandinavia.

00:05:43.408 --> 00:05:44.410
MAHER: Wow, so...

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:47.046
Scandinavians are also much
happier than we are, though.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:50.215
Yeah, well, comparatively, their
women are less happy, so...

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:52.284
-MAHER: I mean...
-(LAUGHTER)

00:05:52.417 --> 00:05:54.052
I know.
I've seen those surveys too.

00:05:54.152 --> 00:05:57.189
I find it a very difficult
thing, to quantify happiness.

00:05:57.323 --> 00:06:00.926
Or, it's kind of like
that pain chart in the hospital.

00:06:01.059 --> 00:06:05.064
One to 10, like somebody's three
is somebody else's eight.

00:06:05.197 --> 00:06:06.198
You know what I mean?

00:06:06.431 --> 00:06:09.835
How happy are they really
in Scandinavia? I don't know.

00:06:09.969 --> 00:06:11.637
-Well, happiness--
-I know this is the greatest

00:06:11.737 --> 00:06:12.804
country in the world,
right, Ted?

00:06:12.905 --> 00:06:14.139
-Amen. Hallelujah.
-(LAUGHTER)

00:06:14.306 --> 00:06:16.108
(LAUGHTER)

00:06:16.208 --> 00:06:17.109
All right, how--

00:06:17.243 --> 00:06:18.177
I'm not gonna get
that one wrong.

00:06:18.311 --> 00:06:20.445
I-- (LAUGHS)

00:06:20.679 --> 00:06:22.448
How will Joe Manchin's
decision--

00:06:22.614 --> 00:06:23.515
ah, this happened today,

00:06:23.615 --> 00:06:25.684
Joe Manchin,
West Virginia senator--

00:06:25.851 --> 00:06:27.553
...decision not
to seek re-election

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:30.056
impact the balance of power
in the Senate?

00:06:30.189 --> 00:06:32.191
I saw a lot
of gleeful Democrats.

00:06:32.324 --> 00:06:33.993
We got rid of Joe Manchin.

00:06:34.226 --> 00:06:38.163
Good luck trying to elect
another Democrat
in West Virginia.

00:06:38.464 --> 00:06:41.601
John Fetterman will win
<i>Dancing with the Stars</i> before--

00:06:41.968 --> 00:06:45.972
Yeah, I mean, he succeeded
Robert Byrd, and that's--

00:06:46.072 --> 00:06:50.242
But, I guess he's making noise
about a third party run.

00:06:51.077 --> 00:06:55.247
I think that he is one of
the several Democrats now

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:58.985
who have decided that something
has to be done, you know,

00:06:59.118 --> 00:07:02.188 line:0%
to offer
an alternative to Biden.

00:07:02.354 --> 00:07:03.222 line:0%
I mean, unfortunately,

00:07:03.322 --> 00:07:05.191 line:0%
Jill Stein
has also jumped into the race,

00:07:05.291 --> 00:07:07.193 line:0%
and that worked out
really well last time.

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:12.831 line:0%
So, but I think that the
balance of power in the Senate
is the bigger threat.

00:07:12.964 --> 00:07:14.166
MAHER: What do you think
about Joe Manchin?

00:07:14.233 --> 00:07:15.100
You must have worked with him--

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:16.168
Well, look,
I like Joe personally.

00:07:16.268 --> 00:07:17.202
He's a very affable guy.

00:07:17.469 --> 00:07:21.073
But he's so much
more conservative
than most Democrats,

00:07:21.173 --> 00:07:22.041
so is there a place--

00:07:22.308 --> 00:07:24.210
His not running means
Republicans will win that seat.

00:07:24.343 --> 00:07:26.011
And in fact,
I would be willing to bet

00:07:26.144 --> 00:07:27.546
-the Democrats won't even--
-No, but could you vote

00:07:27.646 --> 00:07:28.914
for a guy like Joe Manchin?

00:07:29.114 --> 00:07:30.282
-No.
-Never? Really?

00:07:30.416 --> 00:07:31.417
He's not conservative
enough for you?

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:33.185
Because I served with him,
and actually,

00:07:33.285 --> 00:07:35.054
he votes on 90 percent
of the times

00:07:35.154 --> 00:07:36.022
for things that are terrible.

00:07:36.656 --> 00:07:39.024
-So, for example, I watched
the panel discussion you had.
-(LAUGHTER)

00:07:39.191 --> 00:07:41.327
You were talking
about Iran and Hamas,

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:43.930
and I said
at the end of our interview

00:07:44.063 --> 00:07:49.067
that I thought Biden had direct
responsibility for this attack,
and you said why.

00:07:49.201 --> 00:07:50.569
Let me give you one reason why.

00:07:50.703 --> 00:07:55.274
Joe Biden is responsible
for 100 billion dollars
going to the Ayatollah.

00:07:55.474 --> 00:07:57.510
-He made a decision
to do that...
-Okay.

00:07:57.676 --> 00:07:59.378
...and Iran funds Hamas.

00:07:59.478 --> 00:08:01.514
<i> -The Wall Street Journal</i>
 reported in September...
-Uh-huh, right, yes. Okay.

00:08:01.647 --> 00:08:04.717
...they brought Hamas terrorists
to Iran and trained them in Iran

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:06.018
to carry out the attack.

00:08:06.151 --> 00:08:08.387
And I start from
a really simple principle,

00:08:08.554 --> 00:08:11.090
don't give money to terrorists
who want to kill us.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:13.892
-Okay. Right.
-And that, unfortunately,

00:08:14.159 --> 00:08:16.229
the Democrat Party
is on the other side of that.

00:08:17.062 --> 00:08:18.064
It's--

00:08:18.531 --> 00:08:20.765
You went to Harvard,
as you point out often.

00:08:20.932 --> 00:08:22.535
-(LAUGHTER)
-You know-- You know--

00:08:22.635 --> 00:08:23.969
-You know--
-Listen-- Listen, Cornell man.

00:08:24.069 --> 00:08:25.605
You're not exactly
a man of the people.

00:08:25.705 --> 00:08:28.474
Right.
I try not to mention it, though.

00:08:29.141 --> 00:08:31.410
But you know it's more
complicated than that.

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:33.178
-No, I actually
don't think it is.
-Okay.

00:08:33.345 --> 00:08:36.415
When the Ayatollah
chants "death to America"

00:08:36.548 --> 00:08:37.817
-and "death to Israel..."
-MAHER: Yes.

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:38.851
-I believe him.
-MAHER: Right.

00:08:39.151 --> 00:08:40.753 line:0%
MAHER: But what the complicated
part is what I was saying,

00:08:40.853 --> 00:08:43.489 line:0%
the people of Iran
is different than the regime.

00:08:43.589 --> 00:08:44.790 line:0%
But he didn't give the money
to the people.

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:46.225
-MAHER: I--
He gave the money
to the Ayatollah.

00:08:46.359 --> 00:08:48.260
-I...
-The person he's giving it to

00:08:48.393 --> 00:08:52.564
is the one who has pledged
he wants to murder as many Jews

00:08:52.731 --> 00:08:54.166
and as many Americans
as possible.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:57.136
And giving him-- Obama
gave him 100 billion dollars,

00:08:57.269 --> 00:08:59.071
Biden gave him
100 billion dollars,

00:08:59.204 --> 00:09:01.507
and that was
catastrophically foolish.

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:02.908
All right, I'm against that.

00:09:03.075 --> 00:09:05.277
-All right, you created an app--
-See, we've got an agreement.

00:09:05.544 --> 00:09:07.713
No, I mean,
I'm against killing Americans.

00:09:08.147 --> 00:09:10.582
(LAUGHTER, APPLAUSE)

00:09:10.783 --> 00:09:13.085
For--
And I don't care who knows it.

00:09:13.218 --> 00:09:14.120
All right.

00:09:14.253 --> 00:09:16.221
In less than one minute,
because we're on CNN,

00:09:16.388 --> 00:09:19.191
Jordan, you created an app that
teaches people how to write.

00:09:19.324 --> 00:09:22.361
Is that an effective way
to teach them
to be critical thinkers?

00:09:22.461 --> 00:09:23.362
-Yes.
-MAHER: Yes.

00:09:23.562 --> 00:09:26.665
The most-- The most profound
way of learning to think

00:09:26.798 --> 00:09:29.168 line:0%
-is to learn to write,
and edit, in particular.
-MAHER: Yes. I--

00:09:29.335 --> 00:09:31.269 line:0%
Because that's how you get
rid of the stupid ideas.

00:09:31.403 --> 00:09:32.304 line:0%
-MAHER: I agree.
-To answer your--

00:09:32.404 --> 00:09:34.172
There's-- There's three areas.

00:09:34.306 --> 00:09:35.674
There's thinking.

00:09:36.275 --> 00:09:38.944
And then there's talking.
And then there's writing it.

00:09:39.044 --> 00:09:40.813
Each one makes you better.

00:09:41.013 --> 00:09:43.415
-Writing, you really do edit.
-PETERSON: Yeah.

00:09:43.582 --> 00:09:46.986
Talking is a--
is better than just what's just

00:09:47.153 --> 00:09:48.153
-going on in your mind.
-PETERSON: Yeah.

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:49.221
MAHER: That's where people get--

00:09:49.388 --> 00:09:51.123
PETERSON: Other people
edit when you're talking.

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:52.625
-Edit you. Yeah.
-Yes.

00:09:52.725 --> 00:09:56.529
And you don't--
obviously, lots of people
say lots of stupid things,

00:09:56.996 --> 00:09:59.532
but at least you have
some mechanism.

00:09:59.632 --> 00:10:00.967
But what goes on in your mind.

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:03.135
I mean, this is the problem
when people say, you know,

00:10:03.235 --> 00:10:04.436
"God talked to me."

00:10:04.670 --> 00:10:06.071
He didn't. You talked to him.

00:10:06.204 --> 00:10:08.640
He didn't answer.
Those are called thoughts.

